<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Carlow</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Carlow is one of the smallest counties of Ireland, in the province of Leinster, crossed by the rivers Barrow and Slaney. In early medieval times it lay within the lands of the Uí Chennselaig kings of south Leinster. After the Anglo-Norman conquest it became one of the liberties into which the Marshal lordship of Leinster was divided in 1247, while the MacMurrough Kavanaghs held much of it against English rule until the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-carlow.jpg" alt="County Carlow" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest; Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa); Rannpháirtí anaithnid; derivative work by Mabuska · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Island_of_Ireland_location_map_Carlow.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>County Carlow lies in the province of Leinster. It is the second smallest of Ireland's 32 traditional counties by area and the third smallest by population. It is landlocked, and its southern, western and eastern boundaries follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-barrow">River Barrow</a>, the River Slaney and the Blackstairs Mountains. The county is named after the town of Carlow, on the Barrow, which is the county town and largest settlement. The name is an anglicisation of the Irish Ceatharlach; the 19th-century scholar John O'Donovan proposed that it came from Ceatharloch, four lakes, though P. W. Joyce noted that there was no evidence such lakes ever existed. Granite underlies about 70 per cent of the county.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area may have the highest concentration of megalithic monuments per square kilometre in Ireland. The Brownshill Dolmen, a portal tomb about 6,000 years old, has a capstone weighing an estimated 100 tonnes and is reputed to be the heaviest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which gives the county its nickname, the Dolmen County. Carlow contained both the navigable Barrow and the Slighe Cualann, one of the roads leading to Tara, so control of it was vital to anyone claiming the kingship of Leinster. By the 11th century the Mac Murchada branch of the Uí Ceinnselaig had established themselves as Kings of Leinster.</p><p>After the Norman conquest the Borough of Carlow was founded, and in 1247 the independent Liberty of Carlow was established. It was then over three times its present size. In the 14th century it was the capital of the Lordship of Ireland from 1361 to 1374. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-confederate-wars">Irish Confederate Wars</a> of the 1650s most Gaelic Irish and Anglo-Norman landowners were dispossessed, but after the Restoration many Irish nobles petitioned the king and recovered their lands, so the Cromwellian conquest had a limited impact on Carlow. During the War of Independence the Carlow Brigade of the Irish Republican Army used the estate at Duckett's Grove as headquarters from 1919 to 1922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Carlow town holds about 44 per cent of the county's population, which was 61,968 at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Agriculture is strong, notably dairy, cereals and vegetables, and Carlow was historically Ireland's main producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> beet, with the Irish Sugar Manufacturing Company founded there in 1926. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> and Tipperary the county keeps the historic Butler coat of arms, first recorded in 1665.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Carlow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Coriondi appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, placed in the interior of south-eastern Ireland, roughly in modern Counties Kilkenny, <b>Carlow</b> or Wexford, near the Brigantes (Ireland) and the Usdiae.</blockquote><a href="/state/coriondi">Coriondi</a></li><li><blockquote>The five sons of William Marshal all died without heirs, and in 1247 the liberty was divided among his daughters' heirs, forming <b>Carlow</b>, Kilkenny, Wexford, Kildare and part of Laois.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leinster">Lordship of Leinster</a></li><li><blockquote>The Uí Chennselaig, 'descendants of Énna Cennselach', emerged in the 5th and 6th centuries as the leading dynasty of southern Leinster, in modern Wexford and <b>Carlow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ui-chennselaig">Ui Chennselaig</a></li><li><blockquote>The Usdiae appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 in the south-east of Ireland, inland of the coastal Brigantes (Ireland) and Manapii and near the Coriondi, roughly in the region of modern Kilkenny, <b>Carlow</b> or Laois.</blockquote><a href="/state/usdiae">Usdiae</a></li></ul></section>
